# Python environment
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# World Orogen export payloads. Regenerable from the planet code in
# source/worldorogen_seed.txt with the fork's tools/export-planet.mjs; too large
# and too wholesale-rewritten to belong in history. The per-export README.txt
# files are tracked so the format stays documented.
#
# THESE PATTERNS WERE WRITTEN FOR THE OLD FLAT LAYOUT and silently stopped
# matching when source/ became namespaced by build. `source/exoplasim-*/` does
# not match `source/<build>/exoplasim-T42/`, so an entire export payload --
# including 83 MB manifests -- was committed instead. Both depths are matched
# now. Check `git ls-files source/ | wc -l` after adding a build; it should not
# grow by more than the README.txt files.
source/*/raw/
source/*/grid/
source/*/planet.nc
source/*/manifest.json
source/*/*/raw/
source/*/*/grid/
source/*/*/planet.nc
source/*/*/manifest.json
source/maps/*.png
source/*/maps/*.png

# Bulk spectral libraries: gigabytes, redistributed under their own terms, and
# regenerable by download. The same exclude-the-bulk-keep-the-record rule as the
# reference PDFs below; what they ground is written into references/INDEX.md.
references/splib07/
references/poseidon_surface_albedo/
references/*/*.zip

# ExoPlaSim model output: ~20 GB of NetCDF and restart checkpoints. Expensive to
# produce and irreplaceable, but a backup target, not a git one.
# Contents, not the directory itself: git does not descend into an excluded
# directory, so a negation for a file inside one never takes effect.
exoplasim/runs/*
# The index IS tracked. Run ids are UUIDs, so this is the only record of what
# each run was, and it has to survive the output being deleted.
!exoplasim/runs/INDEX.json

# Patched ExoPlaSim build artifacts, produced by
# exoplasim/scripts/build_star_cycle_exoplasim.sh from the vendored source.
exoplasim/inputs/exoplasim_cycle_t42/

# Full climatology fields, ~220 MB, regenerable from exoplasim/runs/ with
# build_climatology.py. The reports, maps, and classification products that
# summarise them are tracked.
exoplasim/analysis/**/*_regular_climatology.nc
exoplasim/analysis/**/*_snapshot_climatology.nc

# Per-region ore prospectivity, a few MB and rebuilt in seconds by
# minerals/scripts/build_prospectivity.py from the export alone. The report
# beside it carries the summary and the provenance and IS tracked. Per build,
# and written as a glob for that reason -- the hydrography rules below were flat
# paths against per-build output and matched nothing for several builds.
minerals/data/*/prospectivity.nc

# The downstream half of the same layer -- weathering, drainage and brine types
# -- on the same terms, and a separate file because it has a different lifetime:
# it reads a climatology and a lake solution, so it is regenerated when either
# moves rather than only when the terrain does. Its report is tracked.
minerals/data/*/downstream_prospectivity.nc

# Per-region derived surface classes: what the surface has BECOME rather than
# what the rock is, on two axes plus their rule bitmasks. 1.2 MB, per build, and
# rebuilt in a minute by pedology/scripts/build_surface_classes.py from a
# climatology, a lake solution, a dust field and the brine solve -- all four of
# which the next carve replaces, so this is among the most disposable artifacts
# in the tree. pedology/analysis/surface_classes_report.json carries the
# fractions, the checks and the provenance, and IS tracked.
pedology/data/*/surface_classes.nc

# Per-region drainage raster, 5.6 MB and rebuilt in seconds by
# hydrography/scripts/build_hydrography.py. The basin table, the coupling
# matrices and the report are small and are tracked.
#
# PER BUILD. These were written as flat paths while the component wrote flat
# output, and were never updated when it moved to data/<source_build>/ -- so
# the rule matched nothing and both rasters were tracked under every build.
hydrography/data/*/regions.nc

# Per-region lakes and rivers, 4.6 MB and rebuilt in seconds by
# hydrography/scripts/surface_water.py once a climatology exists. Same reason
# as regions.nc, and per build for the same reason; the aggregate result is
# tracked in hydrography/analysis/surface_water_report.json.
hydrography/data/*/surface_water.nc

# LPJ-GUESS output: one set of files per gridcell per year, tens of MB per run
# and regenerable from the driver file. The manifest and the analysis products
# that summarise a run are tracked under biosphere/analysis/.
biosphere/runs/
pedology/data/earth_validation_cache/

# ERA5 precipitation at each Earth lake's pour point, cached by
# hydrography/scripts/validate_lake_solver.py so a re-run does not re-fetch 146
# thirty-year series. Delete the directory to refresh it. The result it feeds is
# tracked in hydrography/analysis/lake_solver_validation.json.
hydrography/analysis/cache_era5/
# Bulk reference datasets: large, and redistributable only as their licences
# allow. Hand-extracted tables are the exception and ARE tracked: they are
# kilobytes, they carry their own provenance and validation identities, and
# re-deriving one means re-reading a scanned table by eye. Same reasoning as
# references/INDEX.md below -- exclude the bulk, keep the record.
pedology/data/reference/*
!pedology/data/reference/*.json

# Same rule for hydrography: HydroLAKES, HydroBASINS and GLEV are gigabytes and
# carry their own licences, so the bulk is excluded and the extract is not.
# hydrography/data/reference/README.md records the exact sources and how to
# re-fetch them, and endorheic_lakes.json is the table the validation actually
# reads -- kilobytes, and enough to re-run the test without re-downloading 6 GB.
hydrography/data/reference/*
!hydrography/data/reference/*.json
!hydrography/data/reference/README.md
# Reference PDFs: publisher copyright, many not redistributable. The PDFs are
# excluded; INDEX.md is NOT, because it is the bibliography and the record of
# which sources have actually been read. A blanket `references/` rule used to
# sit here and silently swallowed it.
references/*.pdf
references/*.html
references/_*
references/*.zip
references/ecospeclib*/

# Offline dust fields, rebuilt in minutes by aeolian/scripts/build_dust.py once a
# climatology exists. The JSON report beside them IS tracked: it carries the
# numbers, the brackets and the provenance, and it is kilobytes.
aeolian/analysis/*.nc

# Agent worktrees. Created by the harness under .claude/, one per parallel agent;
# each is a git worktree of this repo and must never be committed into it.
.claude/worktrees/

# Pre-rebuild binaries, kept as the A/B reference arm for the aerocore defect
# patch: rebuild_binaries.py overwrites the executables in place, so once it runs
# the old behaviour is unreproducible. 12 MB of compiled output, so the bulk is
# excluded while SHA256SUMS beside them is NOT -- the same exclude-the-bulk,
# keep-the-record rule as references/INDEX.md. Delete the directory once the
# bit-identical check has been made and recorded.
exoplasim/analysis/prerebuild_binaries_*/*.x
docs/book/

# Profiling beds and perf output for the spectral-transform optimisation work.
# Working copies of a run directory plus per-rank perf.data; GB-scale, and
# rebuilt in a minute by exoplasim/scripts/make_profile_bed.py. The instrument
# and the method note are tracked; what it chews on is not.
exoplasim/bench/

# A worktree cannot see the ignored payloads (source/ exports, compiled model
# binaries), so working in one means symlinking them in from the main checkout.
# The rules above end in "/" and so match directories, not the symlinks that
# stand in for them; without these an accidental `git add -A` would commit
# absolute paths that mean nothing on any other machine.
source/*/*/raw
source/*/*/grid
source/*/*/planet.nc
source/*/*/manifest.json
vendor/orogen/node_modules
